const evidenceAppendix = (post: ResearchPost) => `The evidence should be read as a description of ${post.title}, not as a general claim about every Philippines-based worker or every offshore arrangement. Start with the unit being studied: a ticket, note, record, handoff, approval packet, or controlled test case. Define the time window and the owner of the process before comparing results. Keep the source record attached to the observation, and record when the observation was made. If a system uses a local time zone, state that fact rather than silently converting timestamps. If a reviewer changes a category after the first decision, keep both the original and revised reason so the measurement can be audited.

Facts, analysis, and recommendations should remain separate. A fact is something the selected record or external source establishes. Analysis explains what that fact may mean for the workflow. A recommendation is a decision about what the client should test or change. One should not be used as proof of another. For example, a public description of a control can inform a role boundary, but it cannot prove that this workflow follows the control. A workforce statistic can describe context, but it cannot establish a candidate’s skill or a provider’s result. This separation is especially important when a reader may use the article to make a staffing, access, customer, or publishing decision.

The review should also record what was not observed. Missing logs, unavailable source versions, untested exceptions, and changes after the evidence window all narrow the conclusion. Do not fill those gaps with a confident assumption. Use a safe stop, a named owner, and a next evidence request. When a pattern repeats, compare the cost of repairing the instruction, form, queue, permission, or approval path with the cost of coaching. The correct intervention depends on the cause. Keep the public conclusion modest enough that it remains true if an unexamined edge case appears later. That is the standard used here: a practical finding that another reviewer can reproduce from the stated scope, not a promise about future performance. This boundary keeps the article useful when the queue, reviewer, or source changes.`;

